# Operating rules for AI assistants working in this repo

Project-specific rules, distinct from `docs/` (what the code does) and
`.ai/` (why decisions were made / what's next - read that too).

## Accuracy over confidence

- This is a binary-format project where a wrong byte offset silently
  corrupts a map rather than throwing an error. Never guess opcode
  numbers, struct offsets, or section tags from general StarCraft
  knowledge - read them from `chktrig/triggers.py`, `chktrig/chk.py`,
  etc., and cite the file/line.
- The library is **read-only** (no CHK re-encoding, no PKWARE implode,
  no MPQ repackaging) until the write path lands - see
  `.ai/roadmap.md` item 1. Don't imply a write/edit capability exists
  before it does.
- If a map needed resync recovery on `TRIG` or `MBRF` themselves (not
  just a neighboring section), say so explicitly and treat the decoded
  trigger content as untrusted/possibly-degraded, not ground truth -
  see the Chess map case study in `.ai/context.md` for why.

## Map files

- `maps/*.scm` / `maps/*.scx` are binary test fixtures (real, sometimes
  copyright-protected game maps) - gitignored, never hand-edited, never
  treated as source to reformat or "clean up."
- Any newly added map file worth analyzing is worth a short note in
  `.ai/context.md` (what it is, what's interesting about it), not just
  a silent `git add`.

## Keeping context current

- When a session produces a real finding, decision, or change of
  direction (not just routine implementation), append a dated entry to
  `.ai/context.md`. Don't edit past entries away - add a new one.
- When priorities shift, update `.ai/roadmap.md` directly (it's meant
  to stay current, not accumulate history).

## Note on auto-loading

This file and `.ai/` are conventions, not something Claude Code loads
automatically the way it loads `CLAUDE.md`. If a `CLAUDE.md` exists in
this repo, it should point here explicitly; if it doesn't yet, these
rules only apply when a session is told about them (or reads this file
on its own initiative).
